    11	Abstract
    12	BACKGROUND: A software engineering systematic map is a deﬁned method to build
    13	a classiﬁcation scheme and structure a software engineering ﬁeld of interest. The
    14	analysis of results focuses on frequencies of publications for categories within the
    15	scheme. Thereby, the coverage of the research ﬁeld can be determined. Different
    16	facets of the scheme can also be combined to answer more speciﬁc research
    17	questions.
    18	OBJECTIVE: We describe how to conduct a systematic mapping study in software
    19	engineering and provide guidelines. We also compare systematic maps and
    20	systematic reviews to clarify how to chose between them. This comparison leads to a
    21	set of guidelines for systematic maps.
    22	METHOD: We have deﬁned a systematic mapping process and applied it to complete
    23	a systematic mapping study. Furthermore, we compare systematic maps with
    24	systematic reviews by systematically analyzing existing systematic reviews.
    25	RESULTS: We describe a process for software engineering systematic mapping
    26	studies and compare it to systematic reviews. Based on this, guidelines for
    27	conducting systematic maps are deﬁned.
    28	CONCLUSIONS: Systematic maps and reviews are different in terms of goals, breadth,
    29	validity issues and implications. Thus, they should be used complementarily and
    30	require different methods (e.g., for analysis).
    31	Keywords: Systematic Mapping Studies, Systematic Reviews, Evidence Based Software Engineering
    32	1. INTRODUCTION
    33	As a research area matures there is often a sharp increase in the number of reports and results
    34	made available, and it becomes important to summarize and provide overview. Many research
    35	ﬁelds have speciﬁc methodologies for such secondary studies, and they have been extensively
    36	used in for example evidence based medicine. Until recently this has not been the case in Software
    37	Engineering (SE). However, a general trend toward more evidence based software engineering
    38	(Kitchenham et al. 2004) has lead to an increased focus on new, empirical and systematic
    39	research methods. There have also been proposals for more structured reporting of results, using
    40	for example structured abstracts (Budgen et al. 2007).
    41	The systematic literature review is one secondary study method that has gotten much attention
    42	lately in SE (Kitchenham & Charters 2007, Dyb ˚a et al. 2006, Hannay et al. 2007, Kampenes
    43	et al. 2007) and is inspired from medical research. Brieﬂy, a systematic review (SR) goes through
    44	existing primary reports, reviews them in-depth and describes their methodology and results.
    45	Compared to literature reviews common in any research project, a SR has several beneﬁts: a
    46	well-deﬁned methodology reduces bias, a wider range of situations and contexts can allow more
    47	general conclusions, and use of statistical meta-analysis can detect more than individual studies
    48	in isolation (Kitchenham & Charters 2007). However, SRs also have several drawbacks, the
    49	1
    50
    51	--- Page 2 ---
    52	Systematic Mapping Studies in Software Engineering
    53	main one being the considerable effort required. In software engineering the systematic reviews
    54	have focused on quantitative and empirical studies, but a large set of methods for synthesizing
    55	qualitative research results exists (Dixon-Woods et al. 2005).
    56	Systematic mapping is a methodology that is frequently used in medical research, but that have
    57	largely been neglected in SE. To the best of our knowledge there is only one clear example of
    58	a systematic mapping study within SE (Bailey et al. 2007). This may be due to that systematic
    59	maps have not yet been discovered as a method for aggregating software engineering research.
    60	A systematic mapping study provides a structure of the type of research reports and results that
    61	have been published by categorizing them. It often gives a visual summary, the map, of its results.
    62	It requires less effort while providing a more coarse-grained overview. Previously, systematic
    63	mapping studies in software engineering have been recommended mostly for research areas
    64	where there is a lack of relevant, high-quality primary studies (Kitchenham & Charters 2007).
    65	In this paper we analyze the differences between systematic review and systematic mapping
    66	studies and argue for a broader set of situations where the latter is appropriate. In Section
    67	2 we describe a detailed process for systematic maps. Section 3 summarizes the existing SE
    68	systematic reviews and contrasts them with systematic maps. Section 4 then discusses additional
