   104	2.2. Conduct Search for Primary Studies (All Papers)
   105	The primary studies are identiﬁed by using search strings on scientiﬁc databases or browsing
   106	manually through relevant conference proceedings or journal publications. A good way to create
   107	the search string is to structure them in terms of population, intervention, comparison, and
   108	outcome (Kitchenham & Charters 2007). The structure should of course be driven by the research
   109	questions. Keywords for the search string can be taken from each aspect of the structure. For
   110	example, the outcome of a study (e.g., accuracy of an estimation method) could lead to key words
   111	like ”case study” or ”experiment” which are research approaches to determine this accuracy.
   112	The main difference between the studies is that we do not consider speciﬁc outcomes or
   113	experimental designs in our study (Mujtaba et al. 2008). We avoided this restriction since we
   114	wanted a broad overview of the research area as a whole. If we had only considered certain types
   115	of studies the overview could have been biased and the map incomplete. Some sub-topics might
   116	be over- or under-represented for certain study methods. This difference is also reﬂected in the
   117	search strings:
   118	Object Oriented Design Map: (”object oriented” AND ”design” AND ”empirical evidence”) OR (”OO” AND ”empirical”
   119	AND ”design”) OR (”software design” AND ”OO” AND ”experimental”)
   120	Software Product Line Variability Map: ”software” AND (”product line” OR ”product family” OR ”system family”) AND
   121	(”variability” OR ”variation”)
   122	The choice of databases was also different. For the object oriented design map, all results from
   123	relevant databases for computer science and software engineering were taken into consideration.
   124	However, we only consider the main forums for software product line research, namely the
   125	Software Product Line Conference (SPLC)1and the Workshop on Product Family Engineering
   126	(PFE). Furthermore, we considered journal articles in addition to that. As the SPLC is the main
   127	forum to publish product line research, this is a good starting point to determine the classiﬁcation
   128	scheme and distribution of articles between identiﬁed categories.
   129	2.3. Screening of Papers for Inclusion and Exclusion (Relevant Papers)
   130	Inclusion and exclusion criteria are used to exclude studies that are not relevant to answer the
   131	research questions. The criteria in Table 2 show that the research questions inﬂuenced the
   132	inclusion and exclusion criteria, thus the empirical part is considered only for the object oriented
   133	design map. We found it useful to exclude papers which only mentioned our main focus, variability,
   134	in introductory sentences in the abstract. This was needed since it is a central concept in the
   135	area and thus is frequently used in abstracts without papers really addressing it any further. We
   136	prototyped this technique and did not ﬁnd any misclassiﬁcations because of it.
   137	TABLE 2: Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria
