   158	2.4. Keywording of Abstracts (Classiﬁcation Scheme)
   159	In (Bailey et al. 2007) the process of how the classiﬁcation scheme was created was not clearly
   160	described. For our study, we followed a systematic process shown in Figure 2. Here Keywording
   161	1www.splc.net
   162	3
   163
   164	--- Page 4 ---
   165	Systematic Mapping Studies in Software Engineering
   166	is a way to reduce the time needed in developing the classiﬁcaiton scheme and ensuring that
   167	the scheme takes the existing studies into account. Keywording is done in two steps. First, the
   168	reviewers read abstracts and look for keywords and concepts that reﬂect the contribution of
   169	the paper. While doing so the reviewer also identiﬁes the context of the research. When this
   170	is done, the set of keywords from different papers are combined together to develop a high level
   171	understanding about the nature and contribution of the research. This helps the reviewers deﬁning
   172	a set of categories which is representative of the underlying population. When abstracts are of too
   173	poor quality to allow meaningful keywords to be chosen, reviewers can choose to study also the
   174	introduction or conclusion sections of the paper. When a ﬁnal set of keywords has been chosen,
   175	they can be clustered and used to form the categories for the map.
   176	AbstractKeywordingClassiﬁcation SchemeArticleArticleArticleAbstractAbstractSort Article Into SchemeUpdateScheme
   177	SystematicMap
   178	FIGURE 2: Building the Classiﬁcation Scheme
   179	In our study, three main facets were created. One facet structured the topic (i.e., software
   180	product line variability), for example in terms of architecture variability, requirements variability,
   181	implementation variability, variability management and so forth. Furthermore, the type of
   182	contribution was considered, which for example could be a process, method, tool etc. These
   183	categories were derived from the keywords. However, the research facet which reﬂects the
   184	research approach used in the papers is general and independent from a speciﬁc focus area. We
   185	choose an existing classiﬁcation of research approaches by Wieringa et al. (Wieringa et al. 2006),
   186	summarized in Table 3.
   187	TABLE 3: Research Type Facet
   188	Category Description
   189	Validation Research Techniques investigated are novel and have not yet been implemented in practice.
   190	Techniques used are for example experiments, i.e., work done in the lab.
   191	Evaluation Research Techniques are implemented in practice and an evaluation of the technique is conducted.
   192	That means, it is shown how the technique is implemented in practice (solution
   193	implementation) and what are the consequences of the implementation in terms of
   194	beneﬁts and drawbacks (implementation evaluation). This also includes to identify
   195	problems in industry.
   196	Solution Proposal A solution for a problem is proposed, the solution can be either novel or a signiﬁcant
   197	extension of an existing technique. The potential beneﬁts and the applicability of the
   198	solution is shown by a small example or a good line of argumentation.
   199	Philosophical Papers These papers sketch a new way of looking at existing things by structuring the ﬁeld in
   200	form of a taxonomy or conceptual framework.
   201	Opinion Papers These papers express the personal opinion of somebody whether a certain technique
   202	is good or bad, or how things should been done. They do not rely on related work and
   203	research methodologies.
   204	Experience Papers Experience papers explain on what and how something has been done in practice. It has
   205	to be the personal experience of the author.
   206	We found these categories easy to interpret and use for classiﬁcation without evaluating each
   207	paper in detail (as it is done for a systematic review). For example, evaluation research can be
   208	excluded if no industry cooperation or real world project is mentioned. Furthermore, validation
   209	research is easy to pinpoint by checking whether the paper states hypotheses, uses summary
   210	statistics (e.g., ﬁgures like scatter diagrams or histograms) and describes the main components
   211	of an experimental setup. Furthermore, the scheme allows to classify non-empirical research in
   212	the categories solution proposal, philosophical papers, opinion papers and experience papers. In
   213	4
   214
   215	--- Page 5 ---
   216	Systematic Mapping Studies in Software Engineering
   217	our review, the majority of papers was related to the non-empirical category. Other research type
   218	classiﬁcations have been proposed, and they are discussed in Section 4.
   219	2.5. Data Extraction and Mapping of Studies (Systematic Map)
   220	When having the classiﬁcation scheme in place, the relevant articles are sorted into the scheme,
   221	i.e., the actual data extraction takes place. As shown in Figure 2 the classiﬁcation scheme evolves
   222	while doing the data extraction, like adding new categories or merging and splitting existing
   223	categories. In this step, we used an Excel table to document the data extraction process. The
   224	table contained each category of the classiﬁcation scheme. When the reviewers entered the
   225	data of a paper into the scheme, they provided a short rationale why the paper should be in a
   226	certain category (for example, why the paper applied evaluation research). From the ﬁnal table,
   227	the frequencies of publications in each category can be calculated.
   228	The analysis of the results focuses on presenting the frequencies of publications for each category.
   229	This makes it possible to see which categories have been emphasized in past research and thus to
   230	identify gaps and possibilities for future research. The two maps used different ways of presenting
   231	and analyzing the results.
   232	The object oriented design map is illustrated using summary statistics in form of tables, showing
   233	the frequencies of publications in each category. The intervention type was used in the object
   234	oriented design map to structure the topic and counted the number of papers for each intervention
   235	type. In our study, we used a bubble plot to report the frequencies, shown in Figure 3. This
   236	is basically two x-y scatterplots with bubbles in category intersections. The size of a bubble
   237	is proportional to the number of articles that are in the pair of categories corresponding to the
   238	bubble coordinates. The same idea is used two times, in different quadrants of the same diagram
   239	to show the intersection with the third facet. If a systematic map has more facets than three
   240	additional bubble plots could be added either in the same diagram or by having multiple diagrams
   241	for different facet combinations. We think the bubble plot supports analysis better than frequency
   242	tables. It is easier to consider different facets simultaneously, and summary statistics can still be
   243	added for facets individually. It is also more powerful in giving a quick overview of a ﬁeld, and thus
   244	to provide a map. Further visualization alternatives could be found in statistics, human computer
   245	interaction (HCI) and information visualisation ﬁelds.
