   266	3. COMPARATIVE ANALYSIS AND DISCUSSION
   267	We have studied existing systematic reviews in software engineering and characterized them. This
   268	serves as input to the comparison and discussion in this section. The systematic review studies
   269	were identiﬁed using the following search string: ”systematic review” AND ”software engineering” ,
   270	and by searching Inspec & Compendex, IEEExplore and ACM Digital Library. The search resulted
   271	in a total of 21 papers. We excluded papers that were not in the area of software engineering,
   272	were not based on (Kitchenham & Charters 2007) or did not explicitly state in title or abstract that
   273	they were systematic reviews. This resulted in eight systematic reviews being included. We also
   274	included two further systematic reviews identiﬁed in (Kitchenham 2007) since they also match our
   275	criteria for inclusion. The reference are summarized in the following table.
   276	TABLE 4: Systematic Reviews Included
   277	ID Reference ID Reference
   278	1 (Dyb ˚a et al. 2006) 6 (Mendes 2005)
   279	2 (Grimstad et al. 2006) 7 (Sjøberg et al. 2005)
   280	3 (Hannay et al. 2007) 8 (Jørgensen & Shepperd 2007)
   281	4 (Kampenes et al. 2007) 9 (MacDonell & Shepperd 2007)
   282	5 (Kitchenham et al. 2007) 10 (Davis et al. 2006)
   283	3.1. Characterizing Existing Systematic Reviews
   284	For each of the ten included SE systematic review we characterized them based on their research
   285	goals, criteria for inclusion and exclusion, the number of inclusions and exclusions, classiﬁcation
   286	scheme and means of analysis:
   287	Research Goals: A study that aims to ’Identify Best and Typical Practices’ analyzes a
   288	set of empirical studies to determine which techniques are used and work in practice.
   289	For ’Classiﬁcation and Taxonomy’ a study creates a framework or classiﬁes the existing
   290	research. ’Emphasis on Topic Categories’ means that the study identiﬁes how much
   291	research is published in different sub-topics in the ﬁeld of interest. Finally, a study which
   292	’Identify Publication Fora’, identiﬁes the journals, conferences and workshops relevant in
   293	the focus area.
   294	Inclusion Requirements: Two main inclusion requirements was found: ’Research is within
   295	focus area’ and ’Empirical Methods Used’. In the latter category the included papers used
   296	empirical methods.
   297	Number of Articles Included: In this category we identify the number of ’Potentially relevant
   298	studies’ (i.e., found in the search) and the number of ’Included articles’ (after applying
   299	inclusion and exclusion criteria as well as quality checks).
   300	Means of Analysis: Four types of studies are used: ’Meta studies’ integrate several
   301	studies through statistical analyzes of the studies’ quantitative data. ’Comparative analysis’
   302	uses logical simpliﬁcation and conﬁdence assessment theories. ’Thematic analysis’ counts
   303	papers related to speciﬁc themes or categories. ’Narrative summaries’ focus on qualitative
   304	review and narrative explanations. Further means of analysis are described in (Dixon-Woods
   305	et al. 2005), but we have not found evidence of their use in software engineering systematic
   306	reviews.
   307	The summary of our characterization is shown in Table 5, the reference IDs refer to the studies
   308	in Table 4. It shows that a majority of reviews aim at identifying best practices in software
   309	engineering (Studies 2, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10). Most of these focus on the use of empirical methods
   310	(see inclusion requirements). The remaining studies (1, 3, 4, and 7) put requirements on the
   311	empirical part as they are studying empirical methods in SE. Furthermore, all of the systematic
   312	reviews assess whether papers are related to the focus area. Only two reviews (7,8: (Sjøberg
   313	et al. 2005, Jørgensen & Shepperd 2007)) focused mainly on classiﬁcations and taxonomies
   314	and presented frequencies of papers in identiﬁed categories through thematic analysis. These
   315	two studies also aimed at identifying the relevant publication fora. What distinguishes them from
   316	systematic maps is their in-depth analysis in form of a detailed narrative summary.
