   345	3.2. Comparison
   346	A comparison of systematic maps and reviews was presented already in (Kitchenham &
   347	Charters 2007), focusing mainly on differences in breadth and depth. We extend on that based
   348	the overview of systematic reviews and on experience from conducting systematic maps.
   349	Difference in Goals: When comparing systematic reviews and maps, it is clear that their goals
   350	can be different. As pointed out in (Kitchenham & Charters 2007) a systematic review aims at
   351	establishing the state of evidence, even though other goals like classiﬁcation are mentioned.
   352	However, the systematic reviews we have found focus on identifying best practices based on
   353	empirical evidence (this is the case for the majority of systematic reviews, see Table 5). This is
   354	not a goal for systematic maps, and can not be since they do not study articles in enough detail.
   355	Instead, the main focus here is on classiﬁcation, conducting thematic analysis and identifying
   356	publication fora. Both study types share the aim of identifying research gaps. In our product line
   357	variability map, we identiﬁed gaps by graphing and thus showing in which topic areas and for
   358	which research types there is a shortage of publications. The systematic reviews shows where
   359	particular evidence is missing or is insufﬁciently reported in existing studies. This is not possible
   360	with systematic maps.
   361	Difference in Process: We see two main differences in the process. In maps, the articles are
   362	not evaluated regarding their quality as the main goal is not to establish the state of evidence.
   363	Secondly, data extraction methods differ. For the systematic mapping study, thematic analysis
   364	is an interesting analysis method, as it helps to see which categories are well covered in terms
   365	of number of publications. In systematic reviews, the method of meta analysis requires another
   366	level of data extraction in order to continue working with the quantitative data collected in primary
   367	studies (Kitchenham & Charters 2007). However, we see no reason for why not several different
   368	methods of analysis could be applied in the same study. A thematic summary leading to a map
   369	could be the ﬁrst steps in a more detailed systematic review. We are doing just that based on
   370	(Mujtaba et al. 2008).
   371	Difference in Breadth and Depth: In a systematic mapping study, more articles can be
   372	considered as they don’t have to be evaluated in such detail. Therefore, a larger ﬁeld can be
   373	7
   374
   375	--- Page 8 ---
   376	Systematic Mapping Studies in Software Engineering
   377	structured (e.g., the whole software product line area). This is also reﬂected in the search string
   378	and inclusion criteria that we used in product line variability map. That is, we only considered
   379	population and intervention thus introducing fewer limitations and, potentially, getting more search
   380	hits. On the other hand, the systematic review by Kitchenham et al. (Kitchenham et al. 2007) state
   381	the outcome and quality assessment of the articles as a major focus, which increases the depth
   382	and thus the effort required. This could require a more speciﬁc focus of the study and thus fewer
   383	studies being included. This difference was also recognized in (Kitchenham & Charters 2007).
   384	Classifying the Topic Area: Many reviews have mentioned the lack of methodological rigor
   385	in primary studies, e.g. in (Mendes 2005) ”only 5 % of the studies are considered rigorous
   386	methodologically research” . If we restrict our sample of papers to such a small portion of the
   387	available papers there is a risk that our overview of the topic area will be incomplete. It is likely
   388	that it is also relatively easier to do empirical research in some sub-areas than in others. Thus, a
   389	systematic review focusing on papers using some particular method might introduce a bias when
   390	it comes to presenting the overall research area. This is also supported by the fact that only a
   391	small number of potentially relevant articles is included in the systematic reviews we found above.
   392	Classifying the Research Approach: In our systematic map on software product line variability
   393	we used very high level categories to assess the type of paper in terms of novelty and evaluation.
   394	Due to the argument before, this is valid as no detailed evaluation of articles can be done when
   395	structuring a large area, in consequence the classiﬁcation has to be high level. On the other hand,
   396	a different classiﬁcation scheme should be used for systematic reviews as the empirical research
   397	approach is evaluated in much more detail. Therefore, one review study (Sjøberg et al. 2005)
   398	applied the classiﬁcation scheme proposed by Glass et al (Glass et al. 2002). The scheme
   399	is on a quite detailed level, as it distinguishes more than 22 research methods (like action
   400	research, conceptual analysis, ethnography, ﬁeld study, etc.) and 13 research approaches (for
   401	example descriptive system, evaluative-deductive, evaluative-critical, etc.). In order to judge a
   402	paper regarding these categories requires a much more in-depth analysis of the paper. The high
   403	number of categories for systematic reviews and their detail level is particularly visible for the
   404	reviews (Hannay et al. 2007, Sjøberg et al. 2005).
   405	Validity Consideration: As pointed out in (Mendes 2005) 73 % of the papers were designated
   406	incorrectly, i.e., they for example promised an experiment which was no experiment. The same
   407	problem was reported by (Jørgensen & Shepperd 2007) who found that the term experiment
   408	was not always used in line with the deﬁnition of controlled experiments. Consequently, when
   409	not evaluating the papers in such detail within systematic maps, there might be judgmental
   410	errors when classifying the papers into detailed categories. This threat is minimized in systematic
   411	reviews as here a detailed evaluation of the research methodology is conducted, including
   412	extracting data regarding the methodology (e.g., data collection procedures). This effect can be
   413	somewhat alleviated by the fact that systematic maps can consider more papers than a review
   414	(see above).
   415	Industrial accessibility and relevance: In our contacts with industrial software engineers they
   416	often ask for papers that can give a good introduction to a speciﬁc software engineering area.
   417	Systematic reviews could be good papers to recommend them. When we have done so they often
   418	think the studies are too detailed and hard to access. When presenting the systematic map it was
   419	easier to spark interest. We think the visual appeal of systematic maps can summarize and help
   420	transfer results to practitioners. However, the focus on depth and empirically validated results that
   421	the systematic reviews uncover should be of higher importance for practitioners. Thus, systematic
   422	reviewers should think of ways of presenting and structuring their results in more accessible ways.
   423	4. GUIDELINES FOR SYSTEMATIC MAPS AND REVIEWS IN SOFTWARE ENGINEERING
   424	Based on the comparison above and our experience with systematic reviews and systematic maps
   425	we propose the following extensions to guidelines for these types of studies.
   426	Use Methods Complementarity: We have seen that both methods have different goals that can
   427	also partly contradict each other. For example, a good structure of the topic area is hindered by
   428	excluding the majority of articles due to lack of empirical evidence. Therefore, different search
   429	strategies and inclusion and exclusion criteria have to be applied (as discussed before). A
   430	8
   431
   432	--- Page 9 ---
   433	Systematic Mapping Studies in Software Engineering
   434	systematic map should be used as a ﬁrst step toward a systematic review, i.e., ﬁrst the topic
   435	area is structured and thereafter a speciﬁc focus area is investigated with a systematic review.
   436	However, in this context it is important to mention that a systematic map without conducting a
   437	successive systematic review has a value in itself. It helps to identify research gap in an topic
   438	area and provides indications for lack of evaluation or validation research in certain areas with
   439	little effort.
   440	Adaptive Reading Depth For Classiﬁcation: A common view is that mapping studies are
   441	often conducted based on only the abstracts. However, we have noticed that abstracts are
   442	often misleading and lack important information. As shown in the study by (Budgen et al. 2007)
   443	structured abstracts considerably improve the understandability so we encourage them being
   444	proposed and mandated more widely in software engineering. When they are not available we
   445	propose an adaptive strategy towards the choice of level of detail: do not pre-specify that only
   446	certain parts of a paper can be read. Instead, allow more detailed study of papers for which it
   447	is not clear how they should be classiﬁed. The more parts of a paper one considers the more
   448	effort is required. However, the validity of the results also increases. A mapping study that goes
   449	deeper into the papers can become more like a systematic review. The two type of studies can
   450	be considered as different points on a continuum. Regardless of where on this continuum a study
   451	is designed to be we think that the more quantitative approach common for mapping studies can
   452	complement also systematic reviews.
   453	Classify Papers Based on Evidence and Novelty: Even though one does not evaluate the
   454	research methods in detail, high level classiﬁcation schemes can still be used to classify papers.
   455	The classiﬁcation scheme should also provide categories for non-empirical research. These
   456	requirements are well fulﬁlled by the classiﬁcation scheme presented by (Wieringa et al. 2006)
   457	which we recommend using future systematic maps. A future reﬁnement could be to further divide
   458	it into different classes, e.g. based on evidence level and type of novelty.
   459	Visualize Your Data: When counting the frequencies of publications in speciﬁc categories, one
   460	can determine how well the category is covered. Such information is usually summarized in tables
   461	or visualized using bar plots. However, as we found it interesting to combine different categories
   462	(e.g., map research methods against topic categories) the systematic map bubble plot is more
   463	useful. Bubble plots allow to combine categories with each other and thus the relative emphasis
   464	of research on categories is visible from the plot itself. Therefore, we recommend researchers
   465	doing systematic maps and reviews to investigate and make use of alternative ways of presenting
   466	and visualizing their results. For example, Google’s Visualization Toolkit based on GapMinder2
   467	could be used to create bubble plots that vary over time to better show research trends.
   468	5. CONCLUSION
   469	In this paper we illustrated the systematic mapping process and compared it with systematic
   470	reviews. To do this we have characterized and summarized ten existing systematic reviews in
   471	software engineering. Our ﬁndings are that the study methods differ in terms of goals, breadth
   472	and depth. Furthermore, the use of the methods has different implications for the classiﬁcation of
   473	the topic are and the research approach. As a consequence, both methods should and can be
   474	used complementary. A systematic map can be conducted ﬁrst, to get an overview of the topic
   475	area. Then the state of evidence in speciﬁc topics can be investigated using a systematic review.
   476	Furthermore, based on the comparison and our experience with systematic maps we provided
   477	a set of extensions to guidelines for systematic maps. They speciﬁcally state the importance of
   478	visualizing results; a technique which should be more widely used also in systematic reviews.
   479	In future work more systematic maps should be conducted to gain further experience with our
   480	proposed mapping process and guidelines.
